  "worked_solution": "Let $L$ be the event that a randomly selected student attends a literature course, and let $M$ be the event that they attend a mathematics course. Then, we require $P(M|L),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|L)=\\frac{P(L|M)P(M)}{P(L)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(L|M)=0.5,$ since $50%$ of mathematics students also attend a literature course.\n\n- $P(M)=0.45,$ since $45%$ of students attend a mathematics course.\n\n- $P(L)=0.65,$ since $65%$ of students attend a literature course.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|L) & =\\frac{P(L|M)P(M)}{P(L)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.45)}{0.65} \\\\  & ≈0.35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
